\title{Truncation Orders and Reflection Depths\\ in Tagged Provability Logic}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{11 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study two finite quantitative invariants of theories in a multi-tagged language of
provability: the \emph{inconsistency height}, measured by the least $k$ with
$\vdash \Box_i^k \bot$, and the \emph{reflection depth}, the largest $d$ for which the
rule ``$\vdash \Box_i a$ implies $\vdash a$'' is valid for all formulas $a$ of box depth
$< d$. Two families of consistent theories of the logic of provability GL are analysed
completely. The first is the family $\Lad(c,N)$ of tag-sensitive finite-height ladder
theories, indexed by a height function $c$ and a truncation level $N$; it depends on $c$
only through its depth vector $d_c(i)=\min(N,c(i))$, which is a complete invariant. We
determine the inclusion order on this family exactly. We refute the standing conjecture
that inclusion is governed by pointwise growth of the depth vector together with
preservation of the relative order of the depths, exhibiting a counterexample at $N=2$
with a single explicit separating formula; the conjectured criterion is nonetheless
necessary, and it is sufficient precisely for $N\le 1$. The exact criterion,
\emph{depth domination}, requires pointwise growth together with the condition that a
depth may strictly increase only at a tag of maximal depth; equivalently, the weakenings
of a theory are exactly its uniform truncations, whence they form a chain of length at
most $N+1$. We also identify the tag-indexed criterion with a level-indexed criterion by
a purely arithmetic argument, obtaining a second derivation of the inclusion theorem.
The second family consists of valuated finite-height theories, in particular the block
theories $\Blk(n,w)$, whose reflection depth we compute to be exactly $n-w$ while their
provable iterated boxed falsa are independent of $w$. Combined with the elementary bound
``reflection depth $\le$ inconsistency height'', valid for arbitrary proof systems, this
yields: a consistent GL theory of inconsistency height $n$ and reflection depth exactly
$d$ exists if and only if $d \le n$. In particular the reflection depth is not a function
of the inconsistency spectrum, and depth-$1$ reflection is strictly stronger than minimal
soundness --- an optimal separation. Finally, the block family is rigid: both parameters
are recoverable from the theory, in sharp contrast with the redundancy of the ladder
family.
\end{abstract}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}

A formal theory that can arithmetize its own syntax can express statements about what it
proves. The canonical such statement is $\Con = \neg\Box\bot$. G\"odel's second
incompleteness theorem, in its modal distillation (L\"ob's axiom), says that self-trust
cannot be had for free, but it says nothing about \emph{how much} self-trust a theory has,
nor how the self-trust of one system relates to that of another. Adding several
provability operators $\Box_0,\Box_1,\dots$ --- one per \emph{tag}, i.e.\ per system under
discussion --- makes the transfer statements $\Con_i \to \Con_j$ and their iterated
relatives the natural objects of study.

Earlier work in this line established that, for the tag-sensitive finite-height theories
recalled below, provable transfer is governed exactly by truncated heights, that the
resulting transfer relation is always a total preorder, and that every total preorder
without long strict chains arises this way. What remained open was the \emph{inclusion}
order: given two such theories, when is one a subtheory of the other? A natural conjecture
was recorded; this paper settles it negatively and replaces it with an exact criterion.

A second, independent question concerns two ways of measuring how sound a theory is about
its own provability: the least depth at which it concedes its own inconsistency, and the
complexity threshold up to which its provability claims are correct. In the simplest
models these coincide. We show the coincidence is an artefact of a modelling choice and
compute the whole spectrum of realizable pairs.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Refutation of the order-preservation conjecture for the inclusion order, with an
explicit counterexample at $N=2$ and an explicit separating formula (\S\ref{sec:nec}).
\item The exact inclusion criterion (\emph{depth domination}) and its reformulation:
weakenings are truncations (\S\ref{sec:dd}--\S\ref{sec:trunc}).
\item Structural consequences: weakenings form a chain; there are at most $N+1$ of them;
mutual inclusion is equality (\S\ref{sec:trunc}).
\item The exact threshold of the refuted conjecture: it is correct precisely for $N\le1$
(\S\ref{sec:threshold}).
\item A purely arithmetic bridge to a level-indexed criterion, hence a second, independent
proof of the inclusion theorem (\S\ref{sec:bridge}).
\item The reflection-depth spectrum of the block theories (\S\ref{sec:val}--\S\ref{sec:spectrum}).
\item Exact realizability of the pair (height, reflection depth); independence of the two
invariants; optimal separation of depth-$1$ reflection from minimal soundness
(\S\ref{sec:spectrum}).
\item Rigidity of the block family (\S\ref{sec:rigid}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{The setting}

\subsection{Language}

Fix countably many \emph{tags} $i\in\mathbb{N}$ and \emph{atoms} $p\in\mathbb{N}$.
Formulas are generated by
\[ a \; ::= \; \bot \;\mid\; p \;\mid\; a \to a \;\mid\; \Box_i a . \]
We write $\neg a := a\to\bot$ and $\Con_i := \neg\Box_i\bot$. Iterated boxes:
$\Box_i^0 a = a$, $\Box_i^{k+1}a = \Box_i(\Box_i^k a)$. The \emph{box depth} $\bd(a)$ is
$0$ for $\bot$ and atoms, $\max(\bd(a),\bd(b))$ for $a\to b$, and $1+\bd(a)$ for
$\Box_i a$; thus $\bd(\Box_i^k a) = k + \bd(a)$.

\subsection{Theories}

A \emph{theory} is a proof system $S$ over the formulas with provability relation
$\vdash_S$; $S$ is \emph{consistent} if $\nvdash_S\bot$, and a \emph{GL theory at tag $i$}
if it is closed under modus ponens and necessitation, contains all propositional
tautologies, and contains the distribution axiom
$\Box_i(a\to b)\to(\Box_i a\to\Box_i b)$, the transitivity axiom
$\Box_i a\to\Box_i\Box_i a$, and L\"ob's axiom $\Box_i(\Box_i a\to a)\to\Box_i a$.

\begin{definition}[Minimal soundness]
$S$ is \emph{minimally sound at $i$} if $\nvdash_S \Box_i\bot$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Depth-restricted reflection]
$S$ satisfies \emph{depth-$d$ reflection at $i$}, written $\DR^i_d(S)$, if for every $a$
with $\bd(a)<d$, $\vdash_S \Box_i a$ implies $\vdash_S a$. The \emph{reflection depth} of
$S$ at $i$ is the largest such $d$. The rule is monotone in $d$, and $\DR_0^i(S)$ is
vacuous.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Inconsistency height]
$S$ has \emph{inconsistency height $n$ at $i$} if $\vdash_S \Box_i^k\bot$ exactly for
$k>n$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Two semantics on the ladder \texorpdfstring{$(\mathbb{N},<)$}{(N,<)}}

Both families live on the frame with worlds $\mathbb{N}$, world $m$ accessing exactly the
worlds $n<m$. This frame is transitive and conversely well-founded, which validates
L\"ob's axiom.

\paragraph{Tag-sensitive satisfaction.} Given a \emph{height function}
$c:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$, define $\models^c_m a$ by: $\bot$ false everywhere; atoms true
everywhere; the usual clause for $\to$; and
\[ \models^c_m \Box_i a \iff \bigl(m \le c(i) \Rightarrow \models^c_n a \text{ for all } n<m\bigr). \]
So tag $i$'s accessibility is switched off at all worlds $m>c(i)$, where $i$ is
\emph{dead}. Consequently
\begin{align}
\models^c_m \Box_i \bot &\iff m=0 \ \text{ or } \ c(i)<m, \label{eq:boxbot}\\
\models^c_m \Box_i^k \bot &\iff k\ge1 \ \text{ and } \ \bigl(m<k \ \text{ or } \ c(i)<m\bigr). \label{eq:boxpowbot}
\end{align}

\begin{definition}[Ladder theory]
$\vdash_{\Lad(c,N)} a$ iff $\models^c_m a$ for every $m\le N$.
\end{definition}

$\Lad(c,N)$ is consistent and is a GL theory at every tag.

\begin{proposition}[Inconsistency spectrum]\label{prop:spec}
$\vdash_{\Lad(c,N)}\Box_i^k\bot$ iff $k\ge1$ and $\min(N,c(i))<k$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{definition}[Depth vector]
$d_c(i):=\min(N,c(i))$; by Proposition~\ref{prop:spec} this is the depth of provable
inconsistency of tag $i$ in $\Lad(c,N)$, and $0\le d_c(i)\le N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Completeness of the depth vector]\label{prop:complete}
If $d_c = d_{c'}$ pointwise then $\Lad(c,N)$ and $\Lad(c',N)$ have exactly the same
theorems; if the depth vectors differ, so do the theories.
\end{proposition}

\paragraph{Valuated satisfaction.} Given $V:\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}\to\{0,1\}$, define
$\models^V_m a$ with $\models^V_m p \iff V(m,p)=1$ and
$\models^V_m \Box_i a \iff \models^V_n a$ for all $n<m$ (no tag sensitivity). Let
$\Val(V,N)$ be the theory of the formulas true at all worlds $m\le N$. Taking $V\equiv1$
recovers the tag-blind ladder.

\section{Depth domination}\label{sec:dd}

Fix $N$ and write $d_c(i)=\min(N,c(i))$.

\begin{definition}[Inclusion]
$\Incl(c,c',N)$ means: every theorem of $\Lad(c',N)$ is a theorem of $\Lad(c,N)$ (the
weaker theory is written first).
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Depth domination]
$\DD(c,c',N)$ holds iff
\begin{itemize}
\item[(D1)] $d_c(i)\le d_{c'}(i)$ for all $i$, and
\item[(D2)] for all $i,j$: $d_c(i)<d_{c'}(i)$ implies $d_c(j)\le d_c(i)$.
\end{itemize}
In words: depths may only increase, and a depth may increase strictly only at a tag whose
depth is already maximal.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[The conjectured criterion]
$\CC(c,c',N)$ holds iff (C1) $d_c(i)\le d_{c'}(i)$ for all $i$, and (C2) for all $i,j$:
$d_{c'}(i)\le d_{c'}(j)$ implies $d_c(i)\le d_c(j)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact inclusion criterion]\label{thm:main}
$\Incl(c,c',N) \iff \DD(c,c',N)$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Sufficiency}

\begin{lemma}[Congruence below a level]\label{lem:congr}
Let $M\in\mathbb{N}$ and suppose $n\le c(i)\iff n\le c'(i)$ for all tags $i$ and all
$n\le M$. Then $\models^c_m a \iff \models^{c'}_m a$ for every formula $a$ and every
$m\le M$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $a$. The atomic and implicational cases are immediate. For $\Box_i a$ at
$m\le M$, the flag hypothesis at level $m$ makes the two clauses trigger together, and
the induction hypothesis transports the inner satisfaction at the worlds $n<m\le M$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Dead world]
$m$ is \emph{dead} for $c$ if $m=0$ or $c(i)<m$ for every tag $i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Dead worlds are modally indistinguishable]\label{lem:dead}
At a dead world every box is vacuously true. Hence if $m$ is dead for $c$ and $m'$ is dead
for $c'$, then $\models^c_m a \iff \models^{c'}_{m'} a$ for every $a$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
At a dead world, either there are no accessible worlds or every tag is dead, so every box
is true. Induction on $a$ never needs to descend: atoms are true everywhere, $\bot$ false
everywhere, boxes true at both worlds.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:suff}
$\DD(c,c',N)$ implies $\Incl(c,c',N)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $a$ be a theorem of $\Lad(c',N)$ and $m\le N$. If $m$ is dead for $c$, then since $0$
is dead for $c'$ and $a$ holds there, Lemma~\ref{lem:dead} gives $\models^c_m a$.
Otherwise $m\ne0$ and $m\le c(i_0)$ for some $i_0$. We verify the hypothesis of
Lemma~\ref{lem:congr} at $M=m$: for $n\le m\le N$, $n\le c(i)$ implies
$n\le d_c(i)\le d_{c'}(i)\le c'(i)$ by (D1). Conversely if $n\le c'(i)$ but $n>c(i)$ then
$d_c(i)<n\le d_{c'}(i)$, so (D2) applied to $(i,i_0)$ gives $d_c(i_0)\le d_c(i)<n\le m$,
contradicting $d_c(i_0)\ge m$ (as $i_0$ is alive at $m\le N$). Lemma~\ref{lem:congr} then
gives $\models^c_m a \iff \models^{c'}_m a$, and the latter holds.
\end{proof}

\section{Necessity, and the refutation of the conjecture}\label{sec:nec}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:D1}
$\Incl(c,c',N)$ implies (D1).
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $d_{c'}(i)<d_c(i)$ then $\Box_i^{d_{c'}(i)+1}\bot$ is a theorem of $\Lad(c',N)$ by
Proposition~\ref{prop:spec}, hence of $\Lad(c,N)$, forcing $d_c(i)\le d_{c'}(i)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Order witness]
For tags $i,j$ and $m\ge1$,
\[ W(i,j,m) \;:=\; \Box_i\bot \to \bigl(\neg\Box_j^m\bot \to \neg\Box_j^{m+1}\bot\bigr), \]
read via \eqref{eq:boxpowbot} as: \emph{if tag $i$ is dead here, then tag $j$ does not have
depth exactly $m$ here.}
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:Wprov}
If $1\le m\le c(i)$ then $W(i,j,m)$ is a theorem of $\Lad(c,N)$ for every $N$ and $j$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Suppose $w$ satisfies the two antecedents and refutes the consequent. By
\eqref{eq:boxpowbot}, $\not\models_w\Box_j^m\bot$ gives $w\ge m$ and $w\le c(j)$, while
$\models_w\Box_j^{m+1}\bot$ gives $w<m+1$ or $c(j)<w$; hence $w=m$. By \eqref{eq:boxbot},
$\models_w\Box_i\bot$ gives $m=0$ or $c(i)<m$, both excluded.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:Wfail}
If $1\le m\le N$, $c(i)<m$ and $m\le c(j)$, then $W(i,j,m)$ is refuted at world $m$, hence
is not a theorem of $\Lad(c,N)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
At $w=m$: $\models\Box_i\bot$ since $c(i)<m$; $\not\models\Box_j^m\bot$ since $m\not<m$ and
$c(j)\ge m$; $\models\Box_j^{m+1}\bot$ since $m<m+1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:D2}
$\Incl(c,c',N)$ implies (D2).
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Suppose $d_c(i)<d_{c'}(i)$ and $d_c(i)<d_c(j)$. Put $m:=d_c(i)+1$. Then $m\le N$,
$c(i)<m$, $m\le c(j)$ and $m\le c'(i)$. Lemma~\ref{lem:Wprov} makes $W(i,j,m)$ a theorem
of $\Lad(c',N)$, inclusion transfers it to $\Lad(c,N)$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:Wfail} refutes
it there.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:main} follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:suff} and Lemmas~\ref{lem:D1},
\ref{lem:D2}.

\begin{corollary}[The conjectured criterion is necessary]
$\Incl(c,c',N)$ implies $\CC(c,c',N)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(C1) is (D1). For (C2), assume $d_{c'}(i)\le d_{c'}(j)$. If $d_c(j)=d_{c'}(j)$ then
$d_c(i)\le d_{c'}(i)\le d_{c'}(j)=d_c(j)$; otherwise $d_c(j)<d_{c'}(j)$ and (D2) applied to
$(j,i)$ gives $d_c(i)\le d_c(j)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The counterexample}

\begin{definition}\label{def:cex}
Let $N=2$, $c:=(0,1,1,1,\dots)$ and $c':=(1,2,2,2,\dots)$, so $d_c=(0,1,1,\dots)$ and
$d_{c'}=(1,2,2,\dots)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
$\CC(c,c',2)$ holds: depths increase pointwise ($0\le1$, $1\le2$) and the relative order
of the tags is the same in both vectors.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[Separation]
The formula
\[ W(0,1,1) \;=\; \Box_0\bot \to \bigl(\neg\Box_1\bot \to \neg\Box_1\Box_1\bot\bigr) \]
is a theorem of $\Lad(c',2)$ and is refuted at world $1$ of the $c$-model.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[The order-preservation conjecture is false]
There are $c,c',N$ with $\CC(c,c',N)$ and $\neg\Incl(c,c',N)$; the conjectured criterion is
strictly weaker than inclusion.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
Raising the depth of tag $0$ from $0$ to $1$ \emph{deletes} the witness world $1$ of the
old model --- the unique world at which tag $0$ is dead while tag $1$ is alive at depth
exactly $1$ --- and no world of the new model reproduces that configuration. Conditions
(C1) and (C2) compare only numbers; (D2) compares each raised tag against the maximum
depth, which is exactly the height of the tallest live world.
\end{remark}

\section{Weakenings are truncations}\label{sec:trunc}

\begin{definition}[Top depth]
$T(c,N):=\max_i d_c(i)$, defined unconditionally as the greatest $k\le N$ with
$d_c(i)\ge k$ for some $i$; it is attained.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Truncation theorem]\label{thm:trunc}
\[ \Incl(c,c',N) \iff \exists D\le N\ \forall i,\ d_c(i)=\min\bigl(D,d_{c'}(i)\bigr). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) Take $D:=T(c,N)$, attained at $i_0$. If $d_c(i)=d_{c'}(i)$ then
$\min(D,d_{c'}(i))=d_c(i)$ since $d_c(i)\le D$; if $d_c(i)<d_{c'}(i)$ then (D2) on
$(i,i_0)$ gives $D=d_c(i_0)\le d_c(i)$, so $d_c(i)=D\le d_{c'}(i)$.
($\Leftarrow$) (D1) is clear. A strict increase at $i$ forces $\min(D,d_{c'}(i))<d_{c'}(i)$,
hence $d_c(i)=D$, an upper bound for all $d_c(j)=\min(D,d_{c'}(j))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Chain]
If $\Incl(c_1,c',N)$ and $\Incl(c_2,c',N)$ then $\Incl(c_1,c_2,N)$ or $\Incl(c_2,c_1,N)$:
the weakenings of a fixed theory are linearly ordered by inclusion.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Pigeonhole]
Among any $N+2$ height functions all of whose theories are weaker than a fixed
$\Lad(c',N)$, two generate literally the same theory.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
$\DD$ is reflexive and transitive; mutual inclusion implies equality of depth vectors,
hence of theories; and if $d_c$ is constant equal to $\delta$ with $\delta\le d_{c'}(i)$ for
all $i$, then $\Incl(c,c',N)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
Transitivity of $\DD$ is opaque arithmetically --- composing two (D2) conditions does not
obviously produce a (D2) condition --- but is immediate once (D2) is identified with
inclusion of theories.
\end{remark}

\section{The exact threshold of the refuted conjecture}\label{sec:threshold}

\begin{lemma}
If $N\le1$ then $\CC(c,c',N)$ implies $\DD(c,c',N)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
All depths lie in $\{0,1\}$. If $d_c(i)<d_{c'}(i)$ and $d_c(i)<d_c(j)$ then $d_c(i)=0$,
$d_c(j)=1$, $N=1$, $d_{c'}(i)=1$ is maximal, so $d_{c'}(j)\le d_{c'}(i)$; (C2) on $(j,i)$
gives $d_c(j)\le0$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact threshold]
\[ \bigl(\forall c,c':\ \CC(c,c',N)\Rightarrow\Incl(c,c',N)\bigr) \iff N\le1 . \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) The lemma plus Theorem~\ref{thm:suff}. ($\Rightarrow$) For $N\ge2$ the pair
of Definition~\ref{def:cex} still has depth vectors $(0,1,1,\dots)$ and $(1,2,2,\dots)$,
so $\CC$ holds, while $W(0,1,1)$ separates the theories by
Lemmas~\ref{lem:Wprov},~\ref{lem:Wfail}.
\end{proof}

\section{A second route: level agreement}\label{sec:bridge}

\begin{definition}[Level agreement]
$\LA(c,c',N)$ holds iff for every $m\le N$ that is \emph{alive} for $c$ (i.e.\ $m\le c(i)$
for some $i$) and every tag $j$, $\min(m,c(j))=\min(m,c'(j))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Bridge]
$\LA(c,c',N)\iff\DD(c,c',N)$, by a purely arithmetic argument about truncations.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) The level $d_c(i)$ is alive when positive, and level agreement there forces
(D1); if $d_c(i)<d_{c'}(i)$ while some $j$ is deeper, the level $m:=d_c(i)+1\le N$ is alive
and $\min(m,c(i))=d_c(i)<m=\min(m,c'(i))$.
($\Leftarrow$) At an alive level $m$ witnessed by $i_0$ and a tag $j$: if
$d_c(j)=d_{c'}(j)$ the truncations agree; otherwise (D2) gives
$m\le d_c(i_0)\le d_c(j)$, so both truncations equal $m$.
\end{proof}

Combining the bridge with the level-agreement form of the inclusion theorem reproves
Theorem~\ref{thm:main} by an independent route.

\section{Valuated finite-height theories}\label{sec:val}

The tag-blind ladder makes every atom true everywhere, so box-free formulas are
world-independent; this is precisely why inconsistency height and reflection depth coincide
there. A genuine valuation removes the artefact.

\begin{theorem}
For every valuation $V$, every $N$ and every tag $i$, $\Val(V,N)$ is consistent and is a
GL theory at $i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\bot$ fails at world $0$. Closure under modus ponens and necessitation and validity of
the tautologies and of distribution are pointwise verifications; transitivity holds because
$(\mathbb{N},<)$ is transitive; L\"ob's axiom holds because $(\mathbb{N},<)$ is conversely
well-founded: if $\models^V_n \Box_i a\to a$ for all $n<m$, strong induction on $n<m$ gives
$\models^V_n a$ for all $n<m$, i.e.\ $\models^V_m\Box_i a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Valuation-independence of the inconsistency spectrum]\label{thm:valspec}
$\vdash_{\Val(V,N)}\Box_i^k\bot \iff N<k$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\bot$ contains no atoms, so $\models^V_m\Box_i^k\bot \iff m<k$ by induction on $k$;
requiring this at all $m\le N$ is $N<k$.
\end{proof}

\section{Block valuations, locality, depth probes}

\begin{definition}[Block valuation and block theory]
For a \emph{shift point} $w$, let $V_w(m,p)=1 \iff m<w$, and $\Blk(n,w):=\Val(V_w,n)$.
Under $V_0$ all atoms are false everywhere, so they behave like $\bot$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Locality above the block]\label{lem:local}
If $\bd(a)\le k$ and $w+k\le m$, $w+k\le m'$, then $\models^{V_w}_m a \iff \models^{V_w}_{m'} a$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $a$. Atoms are false at both worlds since $m,m'\ge w$. For $\Box_i b$ with
$\bd(b)\le k-1$: given that $b$ holds below $m$, take $j<m'$; if $j\ge w+(k-1)$ then $b$
cannot distinguish $j$ from $m-1\ge w+(k-1)$, where it holds; if $j<w+k-1\le m$ then $b$
holds at $j$ directly.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Depth probe]
$P(i,k):=\Box_i^k p_0$, of box depth exactly $k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Truth table of the probes]\label{lem:probe}
$\models^{V_w}_m P(i,k) \iff m<w+k$; hence $\vdash_{\Blk(n,w)}P(i,k)\iff n<w+k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $k$: for $k=0$ this is the definition of $V_w$; for $k+1$, $\Box_i P(i,k)$
holds at $m$ iff $j<w+k$ for all $j<m$ iff $m\le w+k$.
\end{proof}

The probe is a \emph{shifted falsum}: for $w=0$ it behaves exactly like $\Box_i^k\bot$.

\section{The reflection-depth spectrum}\label{sec:spectrum}

\begin{theorem}[Reflection holds below the gap]
$\DR^i_{\,n-w}(\Blk(n,w))$ holds for all $n,w,i$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $\bd(a)<n-w$ and let $\Box_i a$ be a theorem; evaluating at world $n$ gives $a$ at all
worlds $<n$. Both $n$ and $n-1$ are $\ge w+\bd(a)$, so Lemma~\ref{lem:local} transfers the
truth of $a$ from $n-1$ to $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reflection fails one step higher]
For $w\le n$, $\DR^i_{\,n-w+1}(\Blk(n,w))$ fails, witnessed by $P(i,n-w)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\Box_i P(i,n-w)=P(i,n-w+1)$ is provable ($n<w+(n-w)+1$), while $P(i,n-w)$ is not
($n \not< n$), by Lemma~\ref{lem:probe}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact reflection depth]
For $w\le n$: $\DR^i_d(\Blk(n,w)) \iff d\le n-w$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Height bounds depth; no semantics required]\label{thm:bound}
If $\vdash_S\Box_i^{n+1}\bot$ and $\nvdash_S\Box_i^n\bot$ then $\DR^i_{n+1}(S)$ fails.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\bd(\Box_i^n\bot)=n<n+1$ and $\Box_i(\Box_i^n\bot)$ is literally $\Box_i^{n+1}\bot$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact realizability of the pair]
There exists a consistent GL theory $S$ at tag $i$ with inconsistency height $n$ (i.e.\
$\vdash_S\Box_i^k\bot$ exactly for $k>n$) and reflection depth exactly $d$ (i.e.\
$\DR^i_{d'}(S)$ exactly for $d'\le d$) \emph{if and only if} $d\le n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Necessity is Theorem~\ref{thm:bound}. Sufficiency is $S:=\Blk(n,n-d)$, using
Theorem~\ref{thm:valspec} and the exact reflection depth.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Independence of the two invariants]
For every $n\ge1$ and every $i$, $\Blk(n,0)$ and $\Blk(n,n)$ prove exactly the same
formulas $\Box_i^k\bot$, yet $\DR^i_n(\Blk(n,0))$ holds while $\DR^i_1(\Blk(n,n))$ fails.
Hence no function of the provable iterated boxed falsa computes the reflection depth.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Optimal separation from minimal soundness]
For every $i$, $\Blk(1,1)$ --- two worlds, atoms true at the root only --- is a consistent
GL theory, minimally sound at $i$, which proves $\Box_i p_0$ and refutes $p_0$; so
$\DR^i_1(\Blk(1,1))$ fails.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
For consistent theories, depth-$1$ reflection implies minimal soundness and the converse
fails. This is optimal, the depth-$0$ rule being vacuous. The separation cannot be
witnessed in the atom-trivial ladders, where box-free formulas are world-independent.
\end{corollary}

\section{Rigidity of the block family}\label{sec:rigid}

\begin{definition}[World guard]
$G(i,j,a):=\Box_i^{j+1}\bot \to \bigl(\neg\Box_i^{j}\bot \to a\bigr)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$\models^V_m G(i,j,a)$ iff ($m=j$ implies $\models^V_m a$); hence
$\vdash_{\Val(V,N)}G(i,j,a) \iff (j\le N \Rightarrow \models^V_j a)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}
If $j<w \iff j<w'$ for all $j\le m$, then $\models^{V_w}_m a \iff \models^{V_{w'}}_m a$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Inclusion criterion for the block family]
Every theorem of $\Blk(n',w')$ is a theorem of $\Blk(n,w)$ iff $n\le n'$ and
$j<w\iff j<w'$ for all $j\le n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) world-by-world congruence. ($\Rightarrow$) If $n'<n$, then
$\Box_0^{n'+1}\bot$ separates. Given $n\le n'$ and $j\le n$ with $j<w$ but $j\ge w'$, the
guard $G(0,j,\neg p_0)$ is a theorem on the right and fails on the left; symmetrically with
$G(0,j,p_0)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Rigidity]
$\Blk(n,w)$ and $\Blk(n',w')$ have the same theorems iff $n=n'$ and $j<w\iff j<w'$ for all
$j\le n$. In particular, if $w,w'\le n$ and the theories coincide then $w=w'$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
For each $n$, the $n+1$ theories $\Blk(n,w)$ with $0\le w\le n$ are pairwise distinct
consistent GL theories with the same inconsistency spectrum, classified by their reflection
depth $n-w$, which takes each value in $\{0,\dots,n\}$ exactly once.
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Depth-domination test.} Given $N$ and the depth vectors on a finite tag set $I$:
reject if $d_c(i)>d_{c'}(i)$ for some $i$; let $T:=\max_{i\in I}d_c(i)$; reject if some $i$
has $d_c(i)<d_{c'}(i)$ and $d_c(i)<T$; else accept. Linear in $|I|$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:trunc} an equivalent test is $d_c=\min(T,d_{c'})$ pointwise, which also
produces the cut level.

\paragraph{Separating formula.} On rejection at the second test with witnesses $i$ (raised,
non-maximal) and $j$ (deeper), output $W(i,j,d_c(i)+1)$; on rejection at the first, output
$\Box_i^{\,d_{c'}(i)+1}\bot$. Every failure of inclusion carries an explicit certificate of
bounded size.

\paragraph{Reflection depth.} For $w\le n$ the reflection depth of $\Blk(n,w)$ is $n-w$,
certified by the probe $\Box_i^{\,n-w}p_0$; over any finite formula set the rule is a direct
check at the worlds $0,\dots,n$.

\section{Discussion}

The conjectured criterion was phrased in the currency of \emph{numbers} --- sizes of depths
and their order --- while inclusion is about which \emph{worlds} exist: which
configurations ``tag $i$ dead, tag $j$ alive at depth exactly $m$'' are realized.
Conditions (C1), (C2) are invariant under order-preserving relabelings of depth values;
inclusion is not, because deleting a world is not an order-theoretic operation on the
depth vector. That the conjecture is correct for $N\le1$ is no accident: with at most two
depth values there is no room for a non-maximal tag to be raised without disturbing the
order, so (C2) accidentally implies (D2); a genuine counterexample needs three depth
values, i.e.\ $N=2$.

The two families behave oppositely for a structural reason. The ladder family varies
\emph{accessibility} with a trivial valuation; accessibility is visible only through the
boxed falsa, which see only truncated heights, so an infinite-dimensional parameter space
collapses onto finitely many theories with a one-parameter inclusion order. The block
family fixes accessibility and varies the \emph{valuation}, which is visible at each
individual world through the world guards, hence full rigidity.

Interpreted, Theorem~\ref{thm:trunc} says that strength profiles cannot be locally
rearranged: within this family the only weakenings are uniform truncations. And the
realizability theorem says that ``how deep does a theory concede its own inconsistency''
and ``how complex a provability claim can it be trusted on'' are genuinely different
measurements coupled by a single inequality; the temptation to read one off the other is
strong precisely because the most familiar examples sit on the diagonal.

\section{Future work}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{The common refinement.} Combine tag-sensitive accessibility with a nontrivial
valuation and classify the resulting theories; the natural conjecture is that per-tag
heights $d$ and per-tag reflection depths $r$ are freely realizable subject only to
$r(i)\le\min(N,d(i))$.
\item \textbf{Restricted transfer.} Determine the provable transfer formulas
$\Box_i^k\bot\to\Box_j^l\bot$ in valuated tag-sensitive theories.
\item \textbf{Inclusion orders in higher families.} Is the ``weakenings form a chain''
phenomenon special to truncation-type families?
\item \textbf{Definable world-configurations.} Order witnesses and world guards are
instances of one pattern: pin a world down, then assert something at it. Characterizing the
definable configurations would explain both classification theorems uniformly.
\item \textbf{Boundary rigidity.} Is there a canonical condition strictly between minimal
soundness and depth-$1$ reflection?
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

We settled the inclusion order on the tag-sensitive finite-height theories: the standing
order-preservation conjecture is false from truncation level $2$ upward and correct below
it, and the exact criterion is depth domination --- equivalently, weakenings are
truncations, so they form a chain of length at most $N+1$. And we separated the two natural
finite invariants of self-trust: the inconsistency height and the reflection depth are
constrained by the single inequality $d\le n$ and by nothing else, every legal pair being
realized by an explicit consistent theory of the logic of provability; within a fixed
height the realizing theories form a rigid scale of $n+1$ pairwise distinct theories indexed
by their reflection depth.

\end{document}

